const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"ISO2_FORMULA_RECALL_001","question":"A frustum of a right circular cone has circular faces of radii $R$ and $r$ (with $R>r$), vertical height $h$ and slant height $l$. Which expression gives its curved (lateral) surface area?","options":{"A":"$\\\\pi (R+r) l$","B":"$\\\\pi (R+r) h$","C":"$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi h}{3}\\\\left(R^{2}+Rr+r^{2}\\\\right)$","D":"$\\\\pi (R+r) l+\\\\pi\\\\left(R^{2}+r^{2}\\\\right)$"},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"microskills":["surface_area_volume","frustum_of_cone"],"common_errors":{"B":"Using the vertical height in place of the slant height — the commonest half-remembering of this result.","C":"Quoting the frustum VOLUME formula when the curved surface area was asked for.","D":"Quoting the TOTAL surface area (curved surface plus the two circular faces) instead of the curved surface alone."},"target_procedural_skill":"FORMULA_RECALL","procedural_skills":["FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"The curved surface of a frustum is the difference of the lateral surfaces of the full cone and the cone cut off, which collapses to $\\\\pi(R+r)l$, where $l$ is the SLANT height. Sanity check: putting $r=0$ turns the frustum back into a cone and the expression becomes $\\\\pi R l$, the standard cone result — the version built on the vertical height would give $\\\\pi R h$, which is wrong for a cone. Nothing is computed here; the item asks only whether the standard result is held correctly."},{"id":"ISO2_FORMULA_RECALL_002","question":"For every convex polyhedron with $V$ vertices, $E$ edges and $F$ faces, which of the following relations always holds?","options":{"A":"$E-V+F=2$","B":"$V-E+F=2$","C":"$V+E-F=2$","D":"$V-E+F=0$"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":40,"microskills":["polyhedra","eulers_formula"],"common_errors":{"A":"Swapping which of $V$ and $E$ carries the minus sign. A cube gives $12-8+6=10$.","C":"Remembering that one of the three terms is subtracted, but subtracting the faces instead of the edges. A cube gives $8+12-6=14$, not 2.","D":"Remembering the alternating pattern but not the constant 2 on the right."},"target_procedural_skill":"FORMULA_RECALL","procedural_skills":["FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Euler's polyhedron formula is $V-E+F=2$. Check on a cube: $V=8$, $E=12$, $F=6$, so $8-12+6=2$. Check on a tetrahedron: $4-6+4=2$. Only $V-E+F=2$ survives both checks."},{"id":"ISO2_FORMULA_RECALL_003","question":"Let $N=2^{4}\\\\times 3^{2}\\\\times 5$. How many positive integers divide $N$ exactly?","options":{},"correct_answer":"30","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"microskills":["number_of_divisors","prime_factorisation"],"common_errors":{"8":"Multiplying the exponents themselves, $4\\\\times 2\\\\times 1$, instead of the exponents each increased by 1.","10":"Adding $(4+1)+(2+1)+(1+1)$ instead of multiplying them.","29":"Applying the rule correctly and then subtracting 1, wrongly excluding $N$ itself as a divisor of $N$."},"target_procedural_skill":"FORMULA_RECALL","procedural_skills":["FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"If $N=p_1^{a_1}p_2^{a_2}\\\\cdots$, the number of positive divisors is $\\\\prod (a_i+1)$, because each divisor chooses an exponent from $0$ to $a_i$ independently. Here $(4+1)(2+1)(1+1)=5\\\\times 3\\\\times 2=30$. The factorisation is handed over, so the only demand is the rule itself; the arithmetic is one product of three small numbers. Verified by machine enumeration of the divisors of $N=720$: exactly 30."},{"id":"ISO2_FORMULA_RECALL_004","question":"Let $p$ be a prime and $a$ a positive integer. The sum of all positive divisors of $p^{a}$ equals","options":{"A":"$\\\\dfrac{p^{a+1}}{p-1}$","B":"$a+1$","C":"$\\\\dfrac{p^{a+1}-1}{p-1}$","D":"$\\\\dfrac{p^{a}-1}{p-1}$"},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"microskills":["sum_of_divisors","geometric_series"],"common_errors":{"A":"Dropping the $-1$ in the numerator of the geometric-series sum. For $p=2,a=3$ this gives 16.","B":"Quoting the formula for the NUMBER of divisors instead of their sum.","D":"Summing the geometric series as though it stopped at $p^{a-1}$, i.e. forgetting that $p^{a}$ is itself a divisor. For $p=2,a=3$ this gives 7 instead of 15."},"target_procedural_skill":"FORMULA_RECALL","procedural_skills":["FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"The divisors of $p^{a}$ are $1,p,p^{2},\\\\ldots,p^{a}$, a geometric progression of $a+1$ terms with ratio $p$, whose sum is $\\\\dfrac{p^{a+1}-1}{p-1}$. Check with $p=2$, $a=3$: divisors $1,2,4,8$ sum to 15, and $\\\\dfrac{2^{4}-1}{2-1}=15$."},{"id":"ISO2_FORMULA_RECALL_005","question":"Let $N=2^{3}\\\\times 3^{2}\\\\times 5^{2}\\\\times 7$. In how many ways can $N$ be written as a product of two positive factors that are coprime to each other? (The same pair written in the two possible orders counts once.)","options":{"A":"16","B":"36","C":"72","D":"8"},"correct_answer":"D","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["coprime_factor_pairs","prime_factorisation"],"common_errors":{"A":"Using $2^{k}$ — the count of ORDERED coprime pairs — and forgetting that the pair $(d, N/d)$ has been counted twice.","B":"Ignoring the coprimality condition and giving the number of ways of writing $N$ as a product of two factors of any kind, $\\\\tfrac{1}{2}\\\\times 72=36$.","C":"Giving the total number of divisors of $N$, $(3+1)(2+1)(2+1)(1+1)=72$."},"target_procedural_skill":"FORMULA_RECALL","procedural_skills":["FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Each prime power must go wholly into one factor or the other, or the two factors would share that prime. With $k$ distinct primes that is $2^{k}$ ordered choices, hence $2^{k-1}$ unordered pairs. Here $k=4$ (the primes 2, 3, 5, 7), so the answer is $2^{3}=8$. Verified by machine: enumerating all 72 divisors $d$ of $N$ and testing $\\\\gcd(d,N/d)=1$ gives 16 ordered solutions, i.e. 8 pairs."},{"id":"ISO2_FORMULA_RECALL_006","question":"A triangle has sides $a$, $b$, $c$, area $\\\\Delta$ and semi-perimeter $s$. The radius of its circumscribed circle is","options":{"A":"$\\\\dfrac{abc}{4\\\\Delta}$","B":"$\\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta}{s}$","C":"$\\\\dfrac{abc}{2\\\\Delta}$","D":"$\\\\dfrac{a+b+c}{4\\\\Delta}$"},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"microskills":["circumradius","inradius","triangle_properties"],"common_errors":{"B":"Quoting the INRADIUS $r=\\\\Delta/s$ — the two radii formulas are the classic pair to swap.","C":"Remembering $abc$ over a multiple of the area but taking the constant as 2 rather than 4.","D":"Replacing the product $abc$ by the perimeter $a+b+c$."},"target_procedural_skill":"FORMULA_RECALL","procedural_skills":["FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"$R=\\\\dfrac{abc}{4\\\\Delta}$. Check on the 3-4-5 right triangle, whose circumradius must be half the hypotenuse, 2.5: $\\\\dfrac{3\\\\cdot 4\\\\cdot 5}{4\\\\cdot 6}=\\\\dfrac{60}{24}=2.5$. The same triangle gives inradius $\\\\Delta/s=6/6=1$, confirming that $\\\\Delta/s$ is the inradius, a different quantity."},{"id":"ISO2_FORMULA_RECALL_007","question":"An equilateral triangle has side 8 cm. Its area, in cm², is","options":{"A":"$16$","B":"$16\\\\sqrt{3}$","C":"$32\\\\sqrt{3}$","D":"$64\\\\sqrt{3}$"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"microskills":["area_of_equilateral_triangle"],"common_errors":{"A":"Using $\\\\tfrac{1}{2}\\\\times \\\\text{base}\\\\times \\\\text{height}$ with the height taken as half the side: $\\\\tfrac{1}{2}\\\\times 8\\\\times 4$.","C":"Using $\\\\tfrac{\\\\sqrt3}{2}a^{2}$ — confusing the area constant with the height constant $\\\\tfrac{\\\\sqrt3}{2}a$.","D":"Remembering only the $\\\\sqrt3\\\\,a^{2}$ part and dropping the division by 4."},"target_procedural_skill":"FORMULA_RECALL","procedural_skills":["FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Area $=\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt3}{4}a^{2}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt3}{4}\\\\times 64=16\\\\sqrt3$ cm². Second route: the height is $\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt3}{2}\\\\times 8=4\\\\sqrt3$, so the area is $\\\\tfrac12\\\\times 8\\\\times 4\\\\sqrt3=16\\\\sqrt3$ cm². The number 64 was chosen so that the division by 4 is exact and no real computation is involved."},{"id":"ISO2_FORMULA_RECALL_008","question":"Find the value of $1^{3}+2^{3}+3^{3}+\\\\cdots+15^{3}$.","options":{},"correct_answer":"14400","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["sum_of_cubes","series_standard_results"],"common_errors":{"120":"Using the sum of the first $n$ natural numbers, $\\\\dfrac{15\\\\times16}{2}=120$, and forgetting to square it.","1240":"Using the sum-of-SQUARES result $\\\\dfrac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}=\\\\dfrac{15\\\\cdot16\\\\cdot31}{6}=1240$.","3375":"Giving $15^{3}$, the last term, instead of the sum."},"target_procedural_skill":"FORMULA_RECALL","procedural_skills":["FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"$\\\\displaystyle\\\\sum_{k=1}^{n}k^{3}=\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\\\right]^{2}$. With $n=15$: $\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{15\\\\times 16}{2}\\\\right]^{2}=120^{2}=14400$. $n=15$ was chosen so the inner value is 120 and the squaring is clean. Confirmed by direct machine summation of the fifteen cubes: 14400."},{"id":"ISO2_FORMULA_RECALL_009","question":"Let $p$ be a prime and $k\\\\ge 2$ an integer. How many of the integers $1,2,3,\\\\ldots,p^{k}$ are coprime to $p^{k}$?","options":{"A":"$p^{k-1}$","B":"$(p-1)^{k}$","C":"$p^{k}-p^{k-1}$","D":"$p^{k}-1$"},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["eulers_totient","coprimality"],"common_errors":{"A":"Counting the multiples of $p$ — the numbers NOT coprime to $p^{k}$ — instead of the ones that are.","B":"Distributing the exponent over $\\\\varphi(p)=p-1$, as if $\\\\varphi$ turned powers into powers.","D":"Treating $p^{k}$ as though it were itself prime, so that everything below it is coprime to it."},"target_procedural_skill":"FORMULA_RECALL","procedural_skills":["FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"The numbers in $1..p^{k}$ that are NOT coprime to $p^{k}$ are exactly the multiples of $p$, of which there are $p^{k}/p=p^{k-1}$. Hence $\\\\varphi(p^{k})=p^{k}-p^{k-1}$. Check with $p=3$, $k=2$: $\\\\varphi(9)=9-3=6$, and indeed 1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 8 are the six numbers coprime to 9 (whereas $p^{k-1}=3$, $p^{k}-1=8$ and $(p-1)^{k}=4$, none of which is 6)."},{"id":"ISO2_FORMULA_RECALL_010","question":"How many consecutive zeros stand at the end of $100!$ when it is written out in full in base 10?","options":{},"correct_answer":"24","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["trailing_zeros","highest_power_of_a_prime"],"common_errors":{"10":"Counting the multiples of 10 up to 100, as though only a factor of 10 could produce a terminal zero.","20":"Counting only the multiples of 5 up to 100 and missing the SECOND factor of 5 contributed by 25, 50, 75 and 100.","21":"Remembering that the higher powers of 5 contribute extra, but adding a single extra 5 for the whole block instead of one for each of 25, 50, 75 and 100."},"target_procedural_skill":"FORMULA_RECALL","procedural_skills":["FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"A terminal zero needs a factor 10 = 2 × 5, and factors of 2 are far more plentiful in $100!$ than factors of 5, so the count is the exponent of 5. By Legendre's rule that exponent is $\\\\left\\\\lfloor \\\\tfrac{100}{5}\\\\right\\\\rfloor+\\\\left\\\\lfloor \\\\tfrac{100}{25}\\\\right\\\\rfloor+\\\\left\\\\lfloor \\\\tfrac{100}{125}\\\\right\\\\rfloor=20+4+0=24$. Verified by machine: $100!$ was computed in full and its trailing zeros counted — 24. Both divisions are trivial; the item is about knowing that the higher powers must be counted at all."},{"id":"ISO2_FORMULA_RECALL_011","question":"Let $N=2^{6}\\\\times 3^{5}\\\\times 5^{4}$. How many divisors of $N$ are perfect squares?","options":{"A":"12","B":"210","C":"105","D":"36"},"correct_answer":"D","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["number_of_divisors","perfect_squares","prime_factorisation"],"common_errors":{"A":"Counting the available even exponents as $3\\\\times 2\\\\times 2$ and forgetting that the exponent 0 is even too, so each count should be one higher.","B":"Giving the total number of divisors, $(6+1)(5+1)(4+1)=210$, ignoring the perfect-square condition.","C":"Assuming half of all divisors are perfect squares and halving 210."},"target_procedural_skill":"FORMULA_RECALL","procedural_skills":["FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"A divisor $2^{x}3^{y}5^{z}$ is a perfect square exactly when $x$, $y$, $z$ are all even. The even values available are $x\\\\in\\\\{0,2,4,6\\\\}$ (4 choices), $y\\\\in\\\\{0,2,4\\\\}$ (3 choices), $z\\\\in\\\\{0,2,4\\\\}$ (3 choices), i.e. $\\\\left(\\\\left\\\\lfloor\\\\tfrac{6}{2}\\\\right\\\\rfloor+1\\\\right)\\\\left(\\\\left\\\\lfloor\\\\tfrac{5}{2}\\\\right\\\\rfloor+1\\\\right)\\\\left(\\\\left\\\\lfloor\\\\tfrac{4}{2}\\\\right\\\\rfloor+1\\\\right)=4\\\\times 3\\\\times 3=36$. Verified by machine: all 210 divisors of $N$ were enumerated and 36 of them are perfect squares."},{"id":"ISO2_FORMULA_RECALL_012","question":"A right pyramid stands on a square base of side $a$, and each of its four triangular faces has slant height $l$. The area of the four triangular faces together is","options":{"A":"$2al$","B":"$4al$","C":"$\\\\tfrac{1}{2}al$","D":"$2al+a^{2}$"},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":50,"microskills":["surface_area_volume","right_pyramid"],"common_errors":{"B":"Taking each triangular face as $a\\\\times l$ rather than $\\\\tfrac12 a l$, i.e. losing the factor $\\\\tfrac12$ that every triangle carries.","C":"Computing one triangular face only instead of all four.","D":"Giving the TOTAL surface area, which adds the square base $a^{2}$ to the four triangles."},"target_procedural_skill":"FORMULA_RECALL","procedural_skills":["FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Each of the four faces is a triangle of base $a$ and height $l$, of area $\\\\tfrac12 al$; four of them give $4\\\\times\\\\tfrac12 al=2al$. Equivalently, lateral surface $=\\\\tfrac12\\\\times(\\\\text{base perimeter})\\\\times(\\\\text{slant height})=\\\\tfrac12\\\\times 4a\\\\times l=2al$."},{"id":"ISO2_FORMULA_RECALL_013","question":"Let $a$, $b$, $c$ be positive integers. Which of the following statements is true for EVERY such $a$, $b$, $c$?","options":{"A":"$\\\\mathrm{LCM}(a,b)=ab$","B":"$\\\\mathrm{HCF}(a,b)\\\\times \\\\mathrm{LCM}(a,b)=ab$","C":"$\\\\mathrm{HCF}(a,b,c)\\\\times \\\\mathrm{LCM}(a,b,c)=abc$","D":"$\\\\mathrm{HCF}(a,b)+\\\\mathrm{LCM}(a,b)=a+b$"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":50,"microskills":["hcf_lcm","number_properties"],"common_errors":{"A":"Using the rule that holds only when $a$ and $b$ are coprime. For $a=4$, $b=6$ the LCM is 12, not 24.","C":"Extending the two-number identity to three numbers. It fails at once: for $a=b=c=2$ the left side is $2\\\\times 2=4$ and the right side is 8.","D":"Remembering that HCF and LCM are tied to $a$ and $b$, but by a sum rather than a product. For $a=4$, $b=6$: $2+12=14\\\\ne 10$."},"target_procedural_skill":"FORMULA_RECALL","procedural_skills":["FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"For two positive integers, each prime contributes its smaller exponent to the HCF and its larger to the LCM, and those two exponents together are the exponents in $a$ and $b$; hence $\\\\mathrm{HCF}(a,b)\\\\times\\\\mathrm{LCM}(a,b)=ab$. No such balancing happens with three numbers, so the three-number version is false ($2,2,2$ is a counterexample; so is $4,6,10$, where $2\\\\times 60=120\\\\ne 240$). the HCF-plus-LCM version fails for $4,6$, and $\\\\mathrm{LCM}(a,b)=ab$ holds only for coprime pairs."},{"id":"ISO2_FORMULA_RECALL_014","question":"A sector of a circle of radius $r$ subtends an angle of $\\\\theta$ degrees at the centre. The perimeter of the sector is","options":{"A":"$2r+\\\\dfrac{\\\\theta}{360}\\\\times \\\\pi r^{2}$","B":"$r+\\\\dfrac{\\\\theta}{360}\\\\times 2\\\\pi r$","C":"$2r+\\\\dfrac{\\\\theta}{360}\\\\times 2\\\\pi r$","D":"$\\\\dfrac{\\\\theta}{360}\\\\times 2\\\\pi r$"},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"microskills":["sector_of_circle","arc_length","perimeter"],"common_errors":{"A":"Adding the sector's AREA to the two radii — mixing the area formula into a perimeter.","B":"Counting only one radius instead of the two that bound the sector.","D":"Giving the arc length alone and forgetting that the boundary of a sector also includes two radii."},"target_procedural_skill":"FORMULA_RECALL","procedural_skills":["FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"The boundary of a sector consists of two radii and the arc. The arc is the fraction $\\\\theta/360$ of the whole circumference $2\\\\pi r$, so the perimeter is $2r+\\\\dfrac{\\\\theta}{360}\\\\times 2\\\\pi r$. Sanity check with $\\\\theta=360$: the expression becomes $2r+2\\\\pi r$, the full circumference plus the doubled radius, as it should for a degenerate 'sector'."},{"id":"ISO2_FORMULA_RECALL_015","question":"A solid hemisphere has radius 6 cm. Its total surface area, in cm², is","options":{"A":"$72\\\\pi$","B":"$144\\\\pi$","C":"$36\\\\pi$","D":"$108\\\\pi$"},"correct_answer":"D","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"microskills":["surface_area_volume","hemisphere"],"common_errors":{"A":"Giving the curved surface $2\\\\pi r^{2}$ only and forgetting the flat circular face — the standard hemisphere slip.","B":"Using $4\\\\pi r^{2}$, the surface of a whole sphere.","C":"Giving the flat circular face $\\\\pi r^{2}$ alone."},"target_procedural_skill":"FORMULA_RECALL","procedural_skills":["FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"A solid hemisphere has a curved surface $2\\\\pi r^{2}$ and a flat circular face $\\\\pi r^{2}$, total $3\\\\pi r^{2}$. With $r=6$: $3\\\\pi\\\\times 36=108\\\\pi$ cm². The whole item turns on whether the flat face is remembered; $36\\\\times 3$ is the only arithmetic."},{"id":"ISO2_FORMULA_RECALL_016","question":"In a triangle whose sides are $a$, $b$ and $c$, the median drawn to the side of length $a$ has length","options":{"A":"$\\\\tfrac{1}{2}\\\\sqrt{2b^{2}+2c^{2}-a^{2}}$","B":"$\\\\tfrac{1}{2}\\\\sqrt{2b^{2}+2c^{2}+a^{2}}$","C":"$\\\\tfrac{1}{2}\\\\sqrt{b^{2}+c^{2}-a^{2}}$","D":"$\\\\tfrac{1}{4}\\\\sqrt{2b^{2}+2c^{2}-a^{2}}$"},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["apollonius_theorem","median_length","triangle_properties"],"common_errors":{"B":"Getting the sign of the $a^{2}$ term wrong — the side the median falls on must be subtracted.","C":"Forgetting that $b^{2}$ and $c^{2}$ are each doubled in Apollonius' theorem.","D":"Halving twice: once inside the theorem $m_a^{2}=\\\\tfrac{2b^{2}+2c^{2}-a^{2}}{4}$ and again outside it."},"target_procedural_skill":"FORMULA_RECALL","procedural_skills":["FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Apollonius' theorem gives $b^{2}+c^{2}=2m_a^{2}+\\\\tfrac{a^{2}}{2}$, so $m_a=\\\\tfrac12\\\\sqrt{2b^{2}+2c^{2}-a^{2}}$. Verified numerically: for the triangle with $a=10$, $b=12$, $c=8$, placing $A=(0,0)$, $B=(8,0)$ and $C$ at the point with $CA=12$, $CB=10$ puts the midpoint of $BC$ at distance $\\\\sqrt{79}\\\\approx 8.8882$ from $A$, and the formula gives $\\\\tfrac12\\\\sqrt{288+128-100}=\\\\tfrac12\\\\sqrt{316}=\\\\sqrt{79}$ — the same value."},{"id":"ISO2_DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION_001","question":"The volume of a frustum of a right circular cone is given by $V=\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi h}{3}\\\\left(R^{2}+Rr+r^{2}\\\\right)$, where $R$ and $r$ are the radii of its two circular faces and $h$ is its vertical height. A frustum has $R=6$ cm, $r=3$ cm and $h=7$ cm. Taking $\\\\pi=\\\\dfrac{22}{7}$, find its volume in cm³.","options":{},"correct_answer":"462","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["surface_area_volume","frustum_of_cone"],"common_errors":{"330":"Substituting $R^{2}+r^{2}=45$ and omitting the middle term $Rr$.","594":"Reading $R^{2}+Rr+r^{2}$ as $(R+r)^{2}=81$.","1386":"Dropping the 3 in the denominator and computing $\\\\pi h\\\\left(R^{2}+Rr+r^{2}\\\\right)$."},"target_procedural_skill":"DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION","procedural_skills":["DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION"],"solution":"$R^{2}+Rr+r^{2}=36+18+9=63$. Then $V=\\\\dfrac{22}{7}\\\\times\\\\dfrac{7}{3}\\\\times 63=\\\\dfrac{22}{3}\\\\times 63=22\\\\times 21=462$ cm³. The formula is handed over, so nothing is recalled and nothing is set up; the 7 and the 63 were chosen so both the $\\\\pi=22/7$ and the division by 3 cancel exactly. Exact check in symbolic form: $\\\\tfrac{1}{3}\\\\pi\\\\cdot 7\\\\cdot 63=147\\\\pi$, and $147\\\\times\\\\tfrac{22}{7}=462$."},{"id":"ISO2_DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION_002","question":"The area of a triangle with sides $a$, $b$, $c$ is $\\\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}$, where $s=\\\\dfrac{a+b+c}{2}$. Find, in cm², the area of a triangle whose sides are 25 cm, 39 cm and 56 cm.","options":{"A":"1092","B":"420","C":"210","D":"700"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":100,"microskills":["herons_formula","area_of_triangle"],"common_errors":{"A":"The same slip with the other pair of sides: $\\\\tfrac12\\\\times 39\\\\times 56$.","C":"Applying Heron's formula correctly and then halving the result, importing the $\\\\tfrac12$ from $\\\\tfrac12\\\\times\\\\text{base}\\\\times\\\\text{height}$.","D":"Ignoring the given formula and computing $\\\\tfrac12\\\\times 25\\\\times 56$, i.e. treating two of the sides as a base and a perpendicular height."},"target_procedural_skill":"DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION","procedural_skills":["DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION"],"solution":"$s=\\\\dfrac{25+39+56}{2}=60$, so $s-a=35$, $s-b=21$, $s-c=4$. Then the product is $60\\\\times 35\\\\times 21\\\\times 4=176400$ and the area is $\\\\sqrt{176400}=420$ cm². Independent check by the cosine rule: $\\\\cos A=\\\\dfrac{39^{2}+56^{2}-25^{2}}{2\\\\cdot 39\\\\cdot 56}$ gives area $\\\\tfrac12 bc\\\\sin A=420$ cm², the same value."},{"id":"ISO2_DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION_003","question":"The perpendicular distance of the point $(x_{0},y_{0})$ from the line $ax+by+c=0$ is $\\\\dfrac{\\\\left|ax_{0}+by_{0}+c\\\\right|}{\\\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}}$. Find the distance of the point $(10,5)$ from the line $3x+4y-15=0$.","options":{},"correct_answer":"7","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["coordinate_geometry","distance_from_a_line"],"common_errors":{"5":"Dividing by $a+b=7$ instead of by $\\\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}=5$.","35":"Evaluating the numerator $|30+20-15|=35$ and forgetting to divide by $\\\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}$ at all.","1.4":"Dividing by $a^{2}+b^{2}=25$, i.e. omitting the square root in the denominator."},"target_procedural_skill":"DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION","procedural_skills":["DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION"],"solution":"Numerator: $|3(10)+4(5)-15|=|30+20-15|=35$. Denominator: $\\\\sqrt{3^{2}+4^{2}}=\\\\sqrt{25}=5$. Distance $=35/5=7$. The 3-4-5 coefficients were chosen so the square root is exact and the division is clean — the only demand is putting four given numbers into a given expression."},{"id":"ISO2_DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION_004","question":"The area of the triangle with vertices $(x_{1},y_{1})$, $(x_{2},y_{2})$ and $(x_{3},y_{3})$ is $\\\\tfrac{1}{2}\\\\left|x_{1}(y_{2}-y_{3})+x_{2}(y_{3}-y_{1})+x_{3}(y_{1}-y_{2})\\\\right|$. Find the area of the triangle whose vertices are $(2,3)$, $(8,11)$ and $(-4,7)$.","options":{"A":"18","B":"4","C":"36","D":"72"},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["coordinate_geometry","area_of_triangle"],"common_errors":{"A":"Halving twice — applying the $\\\\tfrac12$ and then halving the answer again.","B":"Writing the third term as $x_{3}(y_{2}-y_{1})$ instead of $x_{3}(y_{1}-y_{2})$, which gives $|8+32-32|/2=4$.","D":"Evaluating the bracket as 72 and forgetting the factor $\\\\tfrac12$ in front."},"target_procedural_skill":"DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION","procedural_skills":["DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION"],"solution":"$2(11-7)+8(7-3)+(-4)(3-11)=8+32+32=72$, and half of $|72|$ is 36. Independent check by the cross-product form $\\\\tfrac12\\\\left|(x_{2}-x_{1})(y_{3}-y_{1})-(x_{3}-x_{1})(y_{2}-y_{1})\\\\right|=\\\\tfrac12|6\\\\cdot 4-(-6)\\\\cdot 8|=\\\\tfrac12(24+48)=36$."},{"id":"ISO2_DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION_005","question":"A plane cuts a cap of height $h$ from a sphere of radius $R$; the volume of the cap is $V=\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi h^{2}}{3}(3R-h)$. A cap of height 3 cm is cut from a sphere of radius 5 cm. Its volume, in cm³, is","options":{"A":"$108\\\\pi$","B":"$12\\\\pi$","C":"$45\\\\pi$","D":"$36\\\\pi$"},"correct_answer":"D","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["surface_area_volume","sphere","spherical_cap"],"common_errors":{"A":"Omitting the division by 3.","B":"Substituting $h$ where the formula asks for $h^{2}$: $\\\\tfrac{\\\\pi\\\\cdot 3}{3}(15-3)$.","C":"Reading $3R-h$ as $3R$ and dropping the $-h$: $\\\\tfrac{\\\\pi\\\\cdot 9}{3}\\\\times 15$."},"target_procedural_skill":"DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION","procedural_skills":["DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION"],"solution":"$V=\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi\\\\times 3^{2}}{3}\\\\left(3\\\\times 5-3\\\\right)=3\\\\pi\\\\times 12=36\\\\pi$ cm³. Every value needed is given; the answer is left as a multiple of $\\\\pi$ so that no decimal work intrudes."},{"id":"ISO2_DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION_006","question":"For a polygon all of whose vertices are lattice points, Pick's theorem states that its area is $A=I+\\\\dfrac{B}{2}-1$, where $I$ is the number of lattice points strictly inside the polygon and $B$ the number of lattice points on its boundary. A lattice polygon has 96 interior lattice points and 18 boundary lattice points. Find its area, in square units.","options":{},"correct_answer":"104","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":50,"microskills":["picks_theorem","lattice_points","area"],"common_errors":{"105":"Forgetting the $-1$ altogether: $96+9$.","106":"Adding 1 at the end instead of subtracting it.","113":"Adding $B$ rather than $B/2$: $96+18-1$."},"target_procedural_skill":"DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION","procedural_skills":["DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION"],"solution":"$A=96+\\\\dfrac{18}{2}-1=96+9-1=104$ square units. The formula and both counts are given; nothing is left but the substitution."},{"id":"ISO2_DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION_007","question":"A sum invested at compound interest grows to $A=P\\\\left(1+\\\\dfrac{r}{100}\\\\right)^{n}$ after $n$ years, where $P$ is the sum invested and $r$ the annual rate per cent. Find the amount, in rupees, that ₹12,000 becomes after 2 years at 10% per annum compounded annually.","options":{"A":"14520","B":"2520","C":"14400","D":"13200"},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["compound_interest"],"common_errors":{"B":"Giving the interest earned, $A-P=2520$, when the amount was asked for.","C":"Using simple interest, $12000+\\\\tfrac{12000\\\\times 10\\\\times 2}{100}=14400$, and ignoring the formula supplied.","D":"Applying the growth factor once instead of twice: $12000\\\\times 1.1$."},"target_procedural_skill":"DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION","procedural_skills":["DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION"],"solution":"$A=12000\\\\left(1+\\\\tfrac{10}{100}\\\\right)^{2}=12000\\\\times (1.1)^{2}=12000\\\\times 1.21=14520$. Second route, year by year: $12000\\\\to 13200\\\\to 14520$ — the same value."},{"id":"ISO2_DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION_008","question":"It is given that $1^{2}+2^{2}+3^{2}+\\\\cdots+n^{2}=\\\\dfrac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}$. Find the value of $1^{2}+2^{2}+3^{2}+\\\\cdots+30^{2}$.","options":{},"correct_answer":"9455","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["sum_of_squares","series_standard_results"],"common_errors":{"465":"Using $\\\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}$, the sum of the numbers themselves, in place of the formula supplied.","9300":"Reading $2n+1$ as $2n$, giving $\\\\dfrac{30\\\\times 31\\\\times 60}{6}$.","56730":"Substituting correctly but never dividing by 6."},"target_procedural_skill":"DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION","procedural_skills":["DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION"],"solution":"$\\\\dfrac{30\\\\times 31\\\\times 61}{6}=5\\\\times 31\\\\times 61=9455$. Confirmed by direct machine summation of the thirty squares: 9455."},{"id":"ISO2_DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION_009","question":"The sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic progression with first term $a$ and common difference $d$ is $S_{n}=\\\\dfrac{n}{2}\\\\left[2a+(n-1)d\\\\right]$. An arithmetic progression has first term 7 and common difference 4. Find the sum of its first 25 terms.","options":{"A":"103","B":"1375","C":"2750","D":"1425"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["arithmetic_progression","sum_of_ap"],"common_errors":{"A":"Giving the 25th TERM, $a+(n-1)d=7+96=103$, instead of the sum.","C":"Multiplying by $n$ rather than $\\\\tfrac{n}{2}$: $25\\\\times 110$.","D":"Using $nd$ in place of $(n-1)d$: $\\\\tfrac{25}{2}\\\\left[14+100\\\\right]$."},"target_procedural_skill":"DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION","procedural_skills":["DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION"],"solution":"$S_{25}=\\\\dfrac{25}{2}\\\\left[2(7)+24(4)\\\\right]=\\\\dfrac{25}{2}\\\\left[14+96\\\\right]=\\\\dfrac{25}{2}\\\\times 110=25\\\\times 55=1375$. Confirmed by machine-summing the 25 terms $7, 11, 15,\\\\ldots, 103$: 1375."},{"id":"ISO2_DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION_010","question":"For a cyclic quadrilateral with sides $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$ and semi-perimeter $s$, Brahmagupta's formula gives the area as $\\\\sqrt{(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)(s-d)}$. A cyclic quadrilateral has sides 7 cm, 15 cm, 20 cm and 24 cm. Find its area, in cm².","options":{"A":"66","B":"54756","C":"234","D":"117"},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":100,"microskills":["cyclic_quadrilateral","brahmaguptas_formula","area"],"common_errors":{"A":"Giving the perimeter $7+15+20+24=66$ instead of the area.","B":"Evaluating the product $(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)(s-d)=54756$ and forgetting to take the square root.","D":"Halving the correct area, importing a $\\\\tfrac12$ that Brahmagupta's formula does not contain."},"target_procedural_skill":"DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION","procedural_skills":["DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION"],"solution":"$s=\\\\dfrac{7+15+20+24}{2}=33$, so the four factors are $26$, $18$, $13$ and $9$. Their product is $26\\\\times 18\\\\times 13\\\\times 9=54756$, and $\\\\sqrt{54756}=234$ cm². Independent check without Brahmagupta: split the quadrilateral along the diagonal joining the ends of the sides 7 and 15, use the cosine rule on both triangles with supplementary angles; this gives $\\\\cos\\\\theta=-\\\\tfrac35$, $\\\\sin\\\\theta=\\\\tfrac45$, and total area $\\\\tfrac12(7)(15)\\\\tfrac45+\\\\tfrac12(20)(24)\\\\tfrac45=42+192=234$ cm² — the same value, which also confirms that such a cyclic quadrilateral exists."},{"id":"ISO2_DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION_011","question":"The volume of a prismatoid is $V=\\\\dfrac{h}{6}\\\\left(A_{1}+4M+A_{2}\\\\right)$, where $A_{1}$ and $A_{2}$ are the areas of its two parallel end faces, $M$ is the area of the cross-section midway between them, and $h$ is the distance between the ends. Find $V$ when $h=12$ cm, $A_{1}=30$ cm², $A_{2}=18$ cm² and $M=26$ cm².","options":{},"correct_answer":"304","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["prismatoid_formula","surface_area_volume"],"common_errors":{"148":"Dropping the factor 4 on the middle cross-section: $2(30+26+18)$.","608":"Using $h/3$ instead of $h/6$, borrowing the $\\\\tfrac13$ from the cone and pyramid formulas.","1824":"Using $h$ rather than $h/6$: $12\\\\times 152$."},"target_procedural_skill":"DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION","procedural_skills":["DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION"],"solution":"$A_{1}+4M+A_{2}=30+104+18=152$ and $\\\\dfrac{h}{6}=2$, so $V=2\\\\times 152=304$ cm³. Note the middle area 26 was chosen deliberately larger than the mean of the ends, so that the lazy route 'average the two ends and multiply by $h$' gives 288 and is visibly not the answer."},{"id":"ISO2_DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION_012","question":"In any triangle, $a^{2}=b^{2}+c^{2}-2bc\\\\cos A$, where $a$ is the side opposite the angle $A$. In a certain triangle $b=8$ cm, $c=5$ cm and $\\\\cos A=0.5$. Find $a$, in cm.","options":{},"correct_answer":"7","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["cosine_rule","triangle_properties"],"common_errors":{"49":"Stopping at $a^{2}=49$ and reporting it as $a$.","9.43":"Dropping the $-2bc\\\\cos A$ term and computing $\\\\sqrt{64+25}=\\\\sqrt{89}$.","11.36":"Adding the last term instead of subtracting it: $\\\\sqrt{64+25+40}=\\\\sqrt{129}$."},"target_procedural_skill":"DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION","procedural_skills":["DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION"],"solution":"$a^{2}=8^{2}+5^{2}-2(8)(5)(0.5)=64+25-40=49$, so $a=7$ cm. The value of $\\\\cos A$ is supplied precisely so that no trigonometric recall is involved; only the substitution and the final square root of a perfect square remain."},{"id":"ISO2_DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION_013","question":"The count of positive integers not exceeding $n$ and coprime to $n$ is $\\\\varphi(n)=n\\\\prod\\\\left(1-\\\\dfrac{1}{p}\\\\right)$, the product running over the DISTINCT primes $p$ dividing $n$. Given that $8100=2^{2}\\\\times 3^{4}\\\\times 5^{2}$, find $\\\\varphi(8100)$.","options":{"A":"2700","B":"4050","C":"1080","D":"2160"},"correct_answer":"D","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["eulers_totient","coprimality","prime_factorisation"],"common_errors":{"A":"Using only the primes 2 and 3 and forgetting the factor $\\\\left(1-\\\\tfrac15\\\\right)$ for the prime 5.","B":"Applying only the factor for the prime 2: $8100\\\\times\\\\tfrac12$.","C":"Applying $\\\\left(1-\\\\tfrac12\\\\right)$ twice because 2 appears squared, when the product runs over DISTINCT primes only."},"target_procedural_skill":"DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION","procedural_skills":["DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION"],"solution":"$\\\\varphi(8100)=8100\\\\times\\\\left(1-\\\\tfrac12\\\\right)\\\\left(1-\\\\tfrac13\\\\right)\\\\left(1-\\\\tfrac15\\\\right)=8100\\\\times\\\\tfrac12\\\\times\\\\tfrac23\\\\times\\\\tfrac45$. Step by step: $8100\\\\to 4050\\\\to 2700\\\\to 2160$. Confirmed independently by a machine totient computation: $\\\\varphi(8100)=2160$. The factorisation is given, so nothing has to be recalled or derived."},{"id":"ISO2_DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION_014","question":"The area of a regular polygon of $n$ sides, each of length $a$, is $\\\\dfrac{na^{2}}{4}\\\\cot\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{n}$, and for a hexagon $\\\\cot\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{6}=\\\\sqrt{3}$. The area of a regular hexagon of side 6 cm can be written as $k\\\\sqrt{3}$ cm². Find $k$.","options":{},"correct_answer":"54","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["regular_polygon","area","hexagon"],"common_errors":{"9":"Substituting $a$ where the formula asks for $a^{2}$: $\\\\dfrac{6\\\\times 6}{4}$.","27":"Halving the correct result, importing a $\\\\tfrac12$ from the triangle-area habit.","216":"Omitting the division by 4: $6\\\\times 36\\\\times\\\\sqrt3$."},"target_procedural_skill":"DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION","procedural_skills":["DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION"],"solution":"$\\\\dfrac{na^{2}}{4}\\\\cot\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{n}=\\\\dfrac{6\\\\times 36}{4}\\\\times\\\\sqrt3=54\\\\sqrt3$, so $k=54$. Independent check: a regular hexagon of side 6 is six equilateral triangles of side 6, each of area $\\\\tfrac{\\\\sqrt3}{4}\\\\times 36=9\\\\sqrt3$, giving $54\\\\sqrt3$ — the same $k$. The value of the cotangent is supplied so that no trigonometry is recalled."},{"id":"ISO2_DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION_015","question":"The point that divides the segment joining $(x_{1},y_{1})$ and $(x_{2},y_{2})$ internally in the ratio $m:n$ is $\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{mx_{2}+nx_{1}}{m+n},\\\\ \\\\dfrac{my_{2}+ny_{1}}{m+n}\\\\right)$. Find the point dividing the segment joining $(-3,5)$ and $(9,-7)$ internally in the ratio $2:1$.","options":{"A":"$(5,-3)$","B":"$(1,1)$","C":"$(3,-1)$","D":"$(15,-9)$"},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["coordinate_geometry","section_formula"],"common_errors":{"B":"Using the ratio the wrong way round, i.e. pairing $m$ with the first point instead of the second: $\\\\left(\\\\tfrac{9-6}{3},\\\\tfrac{-7+10}{3}\\\\right)$.","C":"Ignoring the ratio and giving the midpoint $\\\\left(\\\\tfrac{-3+9}{2},\\\\tfrac{5-7}{2}\\\\right)$.","D":"Evaluating the numerators $2(9)+(-3)=15$ and $2(-7)+5=-9$ but never dividing by $m+n=3$."},"target_procedural_skill":"DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION","procedural_skills":["DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION"],"solution":"$x=\\\\dfrac{2(9)+1(-3)}{2+1}=\\\\dfrac{15}{3}=5$ and $y=\\\\dfrac{2(-7)+1(5)}{3}=\\\\dfrac{-9}{3}=-3$, so the point is $(5,-3)$. Check: $(5,-3)$ lies on the segment and its distances to the two endpoints are $\\\\sqrt{64+64}$ and $\\\\sqrt{16+16}$, in the ratio $8:4=2:1$."},{"id":"ISO2_DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION_016","question":"The internal bisector of angle $A$ of a triangle, drawn to the side $BC$, has length $t_{a}=\\\\sqrt{bc\\\\left[1-\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{a}{b+c}\\\\right)^{2}\\\\right]}$, where $a$, $b$, $c$ are the sides opposite $A$, $B$, $C$ respectively. Find $t_{a}$ for the triangle with $a=10$, $b=12$ and $c=8$.","options":{"A":"$2\\\\sqrt{30}$","B":"$6\\\\sqrt{2}$","C":"$4\\\\sqrt{3}$","D":"$72$"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["angle_bisector_length","triangle_properties"],"common_errors":{"A":"Adding rather than subtracting inside the bracket: $\\\\sqrt{96\\\\left(1+\\\\tfrac14\\\\right)}=\\\\sqrt{120}$.","C":"Not squaring $\\\\dfrac{a}{b+c}$, which gives $\\\\sqrt{96\\\\left(1-\\\\tfrac12\\\\right)}=\\\\sqrt{48}$.","D":"Evaluating the quantity under the root as 72 and forgetting to take the square root."},"target_procedural_skill":"DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION","procedural_skills":["DIRECT_SUBSTITUTION"],"solution":"$bc=96$ and $\\\\dfrac{a}{b+c}=\\\\dfrac{10}{20}=\\\\dfrac12$, so the bracket is $1-\\\\tfrac14=\\\\tfrac34$ and $t_{a}=\\\\sqrt{96\\\\times\\\\tfrac34}=\\\\sqrt{72}=6\\\\sqrt2$. Independent check with the equivalent standard form $t_{a}=\\\\dfrac{2bc\\\\cos(A/2)}{b+c}$: with $\\\\cos A=\\\\dfrac{144+64-100}{2\\\\cdot 12\\\\cdot 8}=\\\\dfrac{9}{16}$, the half-angle cosine is $\\\\sqrt{\\\\tfrac{1+9/16}{2}}=\\\\tfrac{5}{4\\\\sqrt2}$, and $\\\\dfrac{2\\\\cdot 96}{20}\\\\cdot\\\\dfrac{5}{4\\\\sqrt2}=6\\\\sqrt2$ — the same value."},{"id":"ISO2_NUMERIC_EXECUTION_001","question":"Simplify: $\\\\left(3\\\\sqrt{2}+2\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\right)^{2}$.","options":{"A":"$30+6\\\\sqrt{6}$","B":"$30+12\\\\sqrt{5}$","C":"$30+12\\\\sqrt{6}$","D":"$30$"},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["surds","expansion_of_squares"],"common_errors":{"A":"Writing the cross term as $ab$ instead of $2ab$.","B":"Multiplying the surds by adding what is under the roots: $\\\\sqrt2\\\\times\\\\sqrt3=\\\\sqrt5$.","D":"Expanding as $a^{2}+b^{2}$ only and losing the cross term altogether."},"target_procedural_skill":"NUMERIC_EXECUTION","procedural_skills":["NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"$\\\\left(3\\\\sqrt2\\\\right)^{2}=9\\\\times 2=18$; $\\\\left(2\\\\sqrt3\\\\right)^{2}=4\\\\times 3=12$; cross term $=2\\\\times 3\\\\sqrt2\\\\times 2\\\\sqrt3=12\\\\sqrt6$. Total $=30+12\\\\sqrt6$. Numerical check: $3\\\\sqrt2+2\\\\sqrt3=7.70536\\\\ldots$, whose square is $59.39388\\\\ldots$, and $30+12\\\\sqrt6=59.39388\\\\ldots$ — they agree."},{"id":"ISO2_NUMERIC_EXECUTION_002","question":"Find the value of $\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{5}-2}$.","options":{"A":"$\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{5}+2}{9}$","B":"$\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{5}+2}{3}$","C":"$\\\\sqrt{5}-2$","D":"$\\\\sqrt{5}+2$"},"correct_answer":"D","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"microskills":["surds","rationalising_the_denominator"],"common_errors":{"A":"Computing the new denominator as $\\\\left(\\\\sqrt5\\\\right)^{2}+2^{2}=9$ instead of $\\\\left(\\\\sqrt5\\\\right)^{2}-2^{2}$.","B":"Computing the new denominator as $5-2=3$, forgetting that the 2 is squared as well.","C":"Multiplying numerator and denominator by $\\\\sqrt5-2$ rather than by the conjugate, and then treating the denominator as 1."},"target_procedural_skill":"NUMERIC_EXECUTION","procedural_skills":["NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Multiply above and below by the conjugate $\\\\sqrt5+2$: $\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt5+2}{\\\\left(\\\\sqrt5\\\\right)^{2}-2^{2}}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt5+2}{5-4}=\\\\sqrt5+2$. Numerical check: $\\\\sqrt5-2=0.23607\\\\ldots$ and $1/0.23607=4.23607=\\\\sqrt5+2$."},{"id":"ISO2_NUMERIC_EXECUTION_003","question":"Simplify: $3\\\\sqrt{48}-5\\\\sqrt{27}+2\\\\sqrt{75}$.","options":{"A":"$7\\\\sqrt{3}$","B":"$37\\\\sqrt{3}$","C":"$17\\\\sqrt{3}$","D":"$13\\\\sqrt{3}$"},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["surds","simplifying_radicals"],"common_errors":{"B":"Simplifying each surd correctly but adding all three coefficients, $12+15+10$, and losing the minus sign.","C":"Attaching the minus sign to the last term instead of the middle one: $12+15-10$.","D":"Mis-simplifying $\\\\sqrt{48}$ as $6\\\\sqrt3$ (from $6^{2}=36$) rather than $4\\\\sqrt3$, giving $18-15+10$."},"target_procedural_skill":"NUMERIC_EXECUTION","procedural_skills":["NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"$\\\\sqrt{48}=4\\\\sqrt3$, $\\\\sqrt{27}=3\\\\sqrt3$, $\\\\sqrt{75}=5\\\\sqrt3$. So the expression is $12\\\\sqrt3-15\\\\sqrt3+10\\\\sqrt3=7\\\\sqrt3$. Numerical check: $3(6.9282)-5(5.19615)+2(8.66025)=20.7846-25.9808+17.3205=12.1244$, and $7\\\\sqrt3=12.1244$."},{"id":"ISO2_NUMERIC_EXECUTION_004","question":"Find the value of $\\\\dfrac{2+\\\\sqrt{3}}{2-\\\\sqrt{3}}+\\\\dfrac{2-\\\\sqrt{3}}{2+\\\\sqrt{3}}$.","options":{},"correct_answer":"14","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["surds","rationalising_the_denominator","algebraic_identities"],"common_errors":{"1":"Adding the two fractions by adding the numerators and the denominators separately: $\\\\dfrac{4}{4}$.","2":"Taking the common denominator as $\\\\left(2-\\\\sqrt3\\\\right)\\\\left(2+\\\\sqrt3\\\\right)=4+3=7$ instead of $4-3=1$.","16":"Writing the numerator as $(a+b)^{2}=4^{2}$ and forgetting the $-2ab$ correction in $a^{2}+b^{2}=(a+b)^{2}-2ab$."},"target_procedural_skill":"NUMERIC_EXECUTION","procedural_skills":["NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"The denominators multiply to $\\\\left(2-\\\\sqrt3\\\\right)\\\\left(2+\\\\sqrt3\\\\right)=4-3=1$, so the sum is $\\\\left(2+\\\\sqrt3\\\\right)^{2}+\\\\left(2-\\\\sqrt3\\\\right)^{2}=\\\\left(7+4\\\\sqrt3\\\\right)+\\\\left(7-4\\\\sqrt3\\\\right)=14$. Second route: with $a=2+\\\\sqrt3$ and $b=2-\\\\sqrt3$, $a+b=4$ and $ab=1$, so $\\\\dfrac{a}{b}+\\\\dfrac{b}{a}=\\\\dfrac{a^{2}+b^{2}}{ab}=\\\\dfrac{(a+b)^{2}-2ab}{ab}=\\\\dfrac{16-2}{1}=14$. Decimal check: $3.73205/0.26795+0.26795/3.73205=13.9282+0.0718=14$."},{"id":"ISO2_NUMERIC_EXECUTION_005","question":"Find the value of $117^{2}-83^{2}$.","options":{},"correct_answer":"6800","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"microskills":["difference_of_squares","mental_arithmetic"],"common_errors":{"200":"Stopping at $117+83$ and reporting the sum.","1156":"Subtracting first and then squaring: $(117-83)^{2}=34^{2}$.","40000":"Using the sum twice, $200^{2}$, instead of $(117-83)(117+83)$."},"target_procedural_skill":"NUMERIC_EXECUTION","procedural_skills":["NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"$117^{2}-83^{2}=(117-83)(117+83)=34\\\\times 200=6800$. Direct check: $117^{2}=13689$, $83^{2}=6889$, and $13689-6889=6800$."},{"id":"ISO2_NUMERIC_EXECUTION_006","question":"Find the value of $1235^{2}-1234\\\\times 1236$.","options":{},"correct_answer":"1","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["algebraic_identities","large_number_arithmetic"],"common_errors":{"0":"Assuming the two products must be equal because $1234+1236=2\\\\times 1235$.","2":"Writing $1234\\\\times 1236$ as $1235^{2}-2$ rather than $1235^{2}-1$, i.e. subtracting $n$ terms twice instead of squaring the 1.","-1":"Computing $1234\\\\times 1236-1235^{2}$, the difference the other way round."},"target_procedural_skill":"NUMERIC_EXECUTION","procedural_skills":["NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Write $1234=n-1$ and $1236=n+1$ with $n=1235$. Then $1234\\\\times 1236=n^{2}-1$, so $n^{2}-\\\\left(n^{2}-1\\\\right)=1$. Brute-force check: $1235^{2}=1525225$ and $1234\\\\times 1236=1525224$; the difference is 1."},{"id":"ISO2_NUMERIC_EXECUTION_007","question":"Find the value of $\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{3}{4}+\\\\dfrac{5}{6}-\\\\dfrac{7}{12}\\\\right)\\\\div\\\\dfrac{5}{8}$.","options":{},"correct_answer":"1.6","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["fractions","order_of_operations"],"common_errors":{"1":"Evaluating the bracket correctly as 1 and stopping there, never carrying out the division.","0.625":"Multiplying by $\\\\tfrac58$ instead of dividing by it.","-0.5":"Adding and subtracting the fractions by combining numerators and denominators separately: $\\\\dfrac{3+5-7}{4+6-12}$."},"target_procedural_skill":"NUMERIC_EXECUTION","procedural_skills":["NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Over the common denominator 12: $\\\\dfrac{9}{12}+\\\\dfrac{10}{12}-\\\\dfrac{7}{12}=\\\\dfrac{12}{12}=1$. Dividing by $\\\\tfrac58$ means multiplying by $\\\\tfrac85$, so the value is $\\\\dfrac85=1.6$. Machine check with exact rational arithmetic: $8/5=1.6$."},{"id":"ISO2_NUMERIC_EXECUTION_008","question":"Find the value of $\\\\dfrac{22}{7}\\\\times\\\\left[(10.5)^{2}-(3.5)^{2}\\\\right]$.","options":{},"correct_answer":"308","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["decimals","difference_of_squares","fraction_arithmetic"],"common_errors":{"22":"Subtracting before squaring: taking the bracket as $10.5-3.5=7$.","44":"Doubling instead of squaring — reading $(10.5)^{2}$ as 21 and $(3.5)^{2}$ as 7, so the bracket becomes 14.","616":"Using $(10.5+3.5)^{2}=196$ in place of the difference of the two squares."},"target_procedural_skill":"NUMERIC_EXECUTION","procedural_skills":["NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"$(10.5)^{2}-(3.5)^{2}=110.25-12.25=98$, and $\\\\dfrac{22}{7}\\\\times 98=22\\\\times 14=308$. Faster route: $(10.5-3.5)(10.5+3.5)=7\\\\times 14=98$, then $\\\\dfrac{22}{7}\\\\times 98=308$. Both routes were confirmed with exact rational arithmetic."},{"id":"ISO2_NUMERIC_EXECUTION_009","question":"Find the value of $\\\\sqrt[3]{0.000343}+\\\\sqrt{0.0121}$.","options":{},"correct_answer":"0.18","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["decimals","roots_of_decimals"],"common_errors":{"0.81":"Taking the cube root as 0.7 — misplacing the decimal point by one place, since $0.7^{3}=0.343$, not $0.000343$.","0.117":"Taking the cube root as 0.007, one place too far the other way ($0.007^{3}=0.000000343$).","1.8":"Misplacing the decimal point by one place in BOTH roots, giving $0.7+1.1$."},"target_procedural_skill":"NUMERIC_EXECUTION","procedural_skills":["NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"$343=7^{3}$ and the decimal has 6 places, so the cube root has 2: $\\\\sqrt[3]{0.000343}=0.07$ (check: $0.07^{3}=0.000343$). $121=11^{2}$ and the decimal has 4 places, so the square root has 2: $\\\\sqrt{0.0121}=0.11$ (check: $0.11^{2}=0.0121$). The sum is $0.07+0.11=0.18$."},{"id":"ISO2_NUMERIC_EXECUTION_010","question":"Find the value of $\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{7}+\\\\sqrt{3}}{\\\\sqrt{7}-\\\\sqrt{3}}$.","options":{"A":"$\\\\dfrac{10+\\\\sqrt{21}}{4}$","B":"$\\\\dfrac{5+\\\\sqrt{21}}{2}$","C":"$\\\\dfrac{5+\\\\sqrt{21}}{5}$","D":"$\\\\dfrac{5-\\\\sqrt{21}}{2}$"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["surds","rationalising_the_denominator"],"common_errors":{"A":"Expanding $\\\\left(\\\\sqrt7+\\\\sqrt3\\\\right)^{2}$ as $10+\\\\sqrt{21}$, using $ab$ in place of $2ab$ for the cross term.","C":"Taking the rationalised denominator as $7+3=10$ instead of $7-3=4$.","D":"Multiplying above and below by $\\\\sqrt7-\\\\sqrt3$ — the same expression as the denominator rather than its conjugate — which gives $\\\\dfrac{10-2\\\\sqrt{21}}{4}$."},"target_procedural_skill":"NUMERIC_EXECUTION","procedural_skills":["NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Multiply above and below by $\\\\sqrt7+\\\\sqrt3$: $\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(\\\\sqrt7+\\\\sqrt3\\\\right)^{2}}{7-3}=\\\\dfrac{7+3+2\\\\sqrt{21}}{4}=\\\\dfrac{10+2\\\\sqrt{21}}{4}=\\\\dfrac{5+\\\\sqrt{21}}{2}$. Decimal check: $\\\\dfrac{2.64575+1.73205}{2.64575-1.73205}=\\\\dfrac{4.37780}{0.91370}=4.79129$, and $\\\\dfrac{5+4.58258}{2}=4.79129$."},{"id":"ISO2_NUMERIC_EXECUTION_011","question":"Find the value of $3+\\\\dfrac{1}{2+\\\\dfrac{1}{4+\\\\dfrac{1}{3}}}$.","options":{"A":"$\\\\dfrac{29}{100}$","B":"$\\\\dfrac{60}{19}$","C":"$\\\\dfrac{100}{29}$","D":"$\\\\dfrac{68}{13}$"},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["fractions","continued_fractions","order_of_operations"],"common_errors":{"A":"Obtaining $\\\\dfrac{100}{29}$ and then inverting the whole answer one time too many.","B":"Failing to invert at the middle step: writing $2+\\\\dfrac{1}{13/3}$ as $2+\\\\dfrac{13}{3}=\\\\dfrac{19}{3}$, and then $3+\\\\dfrac{3}{19}$.","D":"Reaching $\\\\dfrac{29}{13}$ correctly but adding it to 3 instead of adding its reciprocal: $3+\\\\dfrac{29}{13}$."},"target_procedural_skill":"NUMERIC_EXECUTION","procedural_skills":["NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Work from the bottom up. $4+\\\\tfrac13=\\\\tfrac{13}{3}$; its reciprocal is $\\\\tfrac{3}{13}$; $2+\\\\tfrac{3}{13}=\\\\tfrac{29}{13}$; its reciprocal is $\\\\tfrac{13}{29}$; finally $3+\\\\tfrac{13}{29}=\\\\tfrac{100}{29}$. Decimal check: $\\\\tfrac{100}{29}=3.44828$, and evaluating the tower numerically gives $3+1/(2+1/4.33333)=3+1/2.23077=3.44828$."},{"id":"ISO2_NUMERIC_EXECUTION_012","question":"Find the value of $7777^{2}-2223^{2}$.","options":{},"correct_answer":"55540000","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["difference_of_squares","large_number_arithmetic"],"common_errors":{"5554":"Giving only $7777-2223$ and never multiplying by the sum $10000$.","55540":"Multiplying 5554 by 10 instead of by 10000 — a place-value slip of three zeros.","100000000":"Using the sum twice: $10000^{2}$."},"target_procedural_skill":"NUMERIC_EXECUTION","procedural_skills":["NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"$7777^{2}-2223^{2}=(7777-2223)(7777+2223)=5554\\\\times 10000=55{,}540{,}000$. The pair was chosen so the sum is exactly $10^{4}$, which is what makes the factorised route far quicker than squaring both numbers. Direct check: $7777^{2}=60481729$, $2223^{2}=4941729$, difference $=55540000$."},{"id":"ISO2_NUMERIC_EXECUTION_013","question":"Find the value of $\\\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\\\dfrac{1}{6}+\\\\dfrac{1}{12}+\\\\dfrac{1}{20}+\\\\dfrac{1}{30}+\\\\dfrac{1}{42}$.","options":{"A":"$\\\\dfrac{5}{6}$","B":"$\\\\dfrac{7}{8}$","C":"$\\\\dfrac{3}{56}$","D":"$\\\\dfrac{6}{7}$"},"correct_answer":"D","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["fractions","addition_of_fractions"],"common_errors":{"A":"Stopping one term early, at $\\\\tfrac{1}{30}$, whose running total is $\\\\tfrac56$.","B":"Running one term too far and including $\\\\tfrac{1}{56}$, which takes the total to $\\\\tfrac78$.","C":"Adding the six numerators over the sum of the six denominators: $\\\\dfrac{6}{112}$."},"target_procedural_skill":"NUMERIC_EXECUTION","procedural_skills":["NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Over the common denominator 420: $\\\\dfrac{210+70+35+21+14+10}{420}=\\\\dfrac{360}{420}=\\\\dfrac{6}{7}$. Second route: each term is $\\\\dfrac{1}{k(k+1)}=\\\\dfrac1k-\\\\dfrac{1}{k+1}$ for $k=1,\\\\ldots,6$, so the sum collapses to $1-\\\\dfrac17=\\\\dfrac67$. Both routes were checked with exact rational arithmetic."},{"id":"ISO2_NUMERIC_EXECUTION_014","question":"Find the value of $\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1}+\\\\sqrt{2}}+\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2}+\\\\sqrt{3}}+\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3}+\\\\sqrt{4}}+\\\\cdots+\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{99}+\\\\sqrt{100}}$.","options":{},"correct_answer":"9","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"microskills":["surds","rationalising_the_denominator","telescoping_sums"],"common_errors":{"10":"Rationalising correctly but reporting $\\\\sqrt{100}$ and forgetting to subtract the surviving $\\\\sqrt1$.","11":"Adding the two surviving terms, $\\\\sqrt{100}+\\\\sqrt1$, instead of subtracting.","99":"Giving the number of terms in the sum."},"target_procedural_skill":"NUMERIC_EXECUTION","procedural_skills":["NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Each term rationalises to $\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{k}+\\\\sqrt{k+1}}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{k+1}-\\\\sqrt{k}}{(k+1)-k}=\\\\sqrt{k+1}-\\\\sqrt{k}$. Summing from $k=1$ to $k=99$, every interior surd cancels against the next, leaving $\\\\sqrt{100}-\\\\sqrt{1}=10-1=9$. Verified by machine: the 99 terms were added in floating point and the total is 9.000000."},{"id":"ISO2_NUMERIC_EXECUTION_015","question":"Find the value of $\\\\left(\\\\sqrt{6}+\\\\sqrt{2}\\\\right)\\\\left(\\\\sqrt{6}-\\\\sqrt{2}\\\\right)\\\\left(\\\\sqrt{3}+1\\\\right)^{2}$.","options":{"A":"$16+8\\\\sqrt{3}$","B":"$16+4\\\\sqrt{3}$","C":"$32+16\\\\sqrt{3}$","D":"$4+2\\\\sqrt{3}$"},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["surds","expansion_of_squares","conjugate_products"],"common_errors":{"B":"Expanding $\\\\left(\\\\sqrt3+1\\\\right)^{2}$ as $3+1+\\\\sqrt3$, using $ab$ rather than $2ab$ for the cross term.","C":"Taking $\\\\left(\\\\sqrt6+\\\\sqrt2\\\\right)\\\\left(\\\\sqrt6-\\\\sqrt2\\\\right)$ as $6+2=8$ instead of $6-2=4$.","D":"Evaluating $\\\\left(\\\\sqrt3+1\\\\right)^{2}$ correctly but treating the conjugate product as 1 and dropping it."},"target_procedural_skill":"NUMERIC_EXECUTION","procedural_skills":["NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"$\\\\left(\\\\sqrt6+\\\\sqrt2\\\\right)\\\\left(\\\\sqrt6-\\\\sqrt2\\\\right)=6-2=4$ and $\\\\left(\\\\sqrt3+1\\\\right)^{2}=3+2\\\\sqrt3+1=4+2\\\\sqrt3$. The product is $4\\\\left(4+2\\\\sqrt3\\\\right)=16+8\\\\sqrt3$. Decimal check: $(2.44949+1.41421)(2.44949-1.41421)(1.73205+1)^{2}=3.86370\\\\times 1.03528\\\\times 7.46410=29.85641$, and $16+8\\\\sqrt3=29.85641$."},{"id":"ISO2_NUMERIC_EXECUTION_016","question":"Find the value of $(0.6)^{3}+(0.4)^{3}+3\\\\times 0.6\\\\times 0.4$.","options":{},"correct_answer":"1","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["decimals","cubes","algebraic_identities"],"common_errors":{"0.28":"Computing the two cubes correctly, $0.216+0.064$, and omitting the third term.","3.28":"Reading $3\\\\times 0.6\\\\times 0.4$ as $3\\\\times(0.6+0.4)=3$.","3.72":"Reading the cubes as 'three times': $3\\\\times 0.6$ and $3\\\\times 0.4$ in place of $0.6^{3}$ and $0.4^{3}$."},"target_procedural_skill":"NUMERIC_EXECUTION","procedural_skills":["NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Straight through: $0.6^{3}=0.216$, $0.4^{3}=0.064$, $3\\\\times 0.6\\\\times 0.4=0.72$; the total is $0.216+0.064+0.72=1$. It comes out exactly because $a+b=1$ makes $a^{3}+b^{3}+3ab=(a+b)^{3}-3ab(a+b)+3ab=1$, but the decimal route needs no such observation. Checked with exact rational arithmetic: the value is exactly 1."}]`);export{e as default};
